{{also|Crane|crâne|crâné}}
==English==
{{wp|+ (bird)}}
{{wp|+ (machine)}}
[[Image:Журавель_сірий_(Grus_grus)_на_болоті.jpg|thumb|right|[[common crane|Common crane]] ({{taxfmt|Grus grus|species}}) (1)]]
[[Image:TowerCrane.jpg|thumb|right|A mechanical crane (3)]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kɹeɪn/|a=RP,GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-crane.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/kɹæɪn/|a=AU,NZ}}
* {{IPA|en|/kɾen/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/kɾeːn/|a=Wales,non-pane-pain}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪn|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|crane}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cran|t=crane}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*kran}}, {{m|gmw-pro|*kranō}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*kranô|t=crane}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gerh₂-|t=to cry hoarsely}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|cran|t=crane}}, {{cog|nl|kraan|t=crane}}, {{cog|nds-de|Kroon|t=crane}}, {{cog|de|Kran|t=crane}}. The mechanical devices are named from their likeness to the bird.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q25365}}[[any|Any]] [[bird]] of the [[family]] {{taxfmt|Gruidae|family}}, [[large]] [[bird]]s with [[long]] [[leg]]s and a [[long]] [[neck]] which is [[extend]]ed during [[flight]].
#* '''1876''', "[[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, Ninth Edition/Burmah|Burmah]]" in the [[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, Ninth Edition| ''Encyclopædia Britannica'', 9th ed.]], [[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, Ninth Edition/Volume IV|Vol. IV]], [[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, Ninth Edition/Burmah|p. 552]]:
#*: Aquatic birds of various kinds are very numerous, such as geese, [[darter]]s (''[[Anhinga melanogaster|Flotus melanogaster]]''), [[scissor-bill]]s ({{taxfmt|Rhynchops nigra|species}}), [[adjutant]]s ({{taxfmt|Leptoptilos argala|species}}), pelicans, [[cormorant]]s, [[crane]]s ({{taxfmt|Grus antigone|species}}, in [[Burmese]] ''gyoja''), [[whimbrel]]s, plovers, and ibises.
# {{lb|en|US|_|dialectal}} {{taxfmt|Ardea herodias|species}}, the [[great blue heron]].
# A [[mechanical]] [[lift]]ing [[machine]] or [[device]], often used for lifting heavy loads for [[industrial]] or [[construction]] purposes.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Bob Foster|title=Birdum or Bust!|publisher=Seaview Press| location=Henley Beach, SA| page=111| text=Large '''cranes''' were virtually non-existent in the areas I worked with this truck, so we jacked everything on and off[.]}}
# An iron arm with horizontal motion, attached to the side or back of a fireplace for supporting kettles etc. over the fire.
# A siphon, or bent pipe, for drawing liquors out of a cask.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A forked post or projecting bracket to support spars, etc.; generally used in pairs.

