====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[extend]] (one's neck).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1879|author=w:George Eliot|title=w:Impressions of Theophrastus Such
|passage=and my bachelor's hearth is imbedded where by much craning of head and neck I can catch sight of a sycamore in the Square garden,}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1948|month=November and December|title=By Broad Gauge to Cornwall|journal=Railway Magazine|page=357|text=Didcot had one definite pleasure. We knew that little boys would be going up and down the platform singing out, "Banbury cakes! Banbury cakes!" And mother would '''crane''' out and buy some, just to encourage the crew.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|passage=I'm gonna be a star and people will '''crane''' necks<br>To get a glimpse of me and see if I am having sex|title={{w|Troublemaker (Weezer song)|Troublemaker}}|year=2008|artist=w:Weezer|album=w:Weezer (Red Album)|author=w:Rivers Cuomo}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[raise]] or [[lower]] with, or as if with, a [[crane#Noun|crane]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|William Bates (minister)|William Bates}}|year=1693|title=Sermons preach'd on Several Occasions|passage= What engines, what instruments are used in '''craning''' up a soul, sunk below the centre, to the highest heavens.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1619|author=w:Philip Massinger; w:Nathan Field|title=The Fatal Dowry
|passage=an upstart '''craned''' up to the height he has}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2024 January 24|author=Peter Plisner|title=Rising to the University challenge|journal=RAIL|issue=1001|pages=60-61|text=Several overnight weekend possessions were required to '''crane''' in new bridges and various other parts of the buildings which were manufactured offsite.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[pull up]] before a [[jump]].

